 "rulebook": "World Athletics Competition and Technical Rules 2024: TR 32.9 (the javelin runway and its arc), TR 32.12 (the sector), TR 32.14, 32.16, 32.17 and 32.20 (throwing events: failures, the landing, leaving the runway, the measurement), TR 38.1 (the throw), TR 38.3-38.6 (the men's javelin, its grip)",
 "deviations": [
  "TR 38.1(a) (\"the javelin shall be held at the grip with one hand\"): the hand is a two-jaw gripper, Unitree's Dex1-1 (90 mm stroke, 80 mm jaws, at most 120 N of clamp, opening at most 375 mm/s; Unitree sells it for the G1, the H2 and the R1, not the H1-2: here it sits on the H1-2's right wrist where the palm ends); the cord lies across its jaws and is held by their friction only. Held means both jaws touching the cord; the hold may break for at most 0.02 s (contacts flicker); the release is the end of the hold, the last moment both jaws touched the cord. \"Over the shoulder or upper part of the throwing arm\" is judged at the release: the javelin's centre of gravity must then be at least 0.0 m higher than the throwing shoulder's joint.",
  "TR 38.1(a) (one hand): before the release the javelin may not touch your left arm or hand (it may rest against the rest of you: the rule book forbids only the second hand).",
  "TR 38.1(c): \"turned completely around\" is judged as the chest's heading more than 135.0 degrees away from the throwing direction at any time before the release.",
  "TR 38.3-38.6: the javelin is one rigid body (no bending or vibration) whose head does not stick in the ground; the mark is the point where the head first touches the ground; the cord grip is a smooth band 0.16 m long and 0.029 m across (no pattern: the pads' rubber gives the friction).",
  "Flight: quasi-steady aerodynamics (MuJoCo's ellipsoid fluid model with coefficients of the order of the published wind-tunnel data, the centre of pressure 0.25 m behind the centre of gravity), still air, no wind. The model's drag does not grow with the angle of attack as a real javelin's does: a javelin released nose-high flies up to a fifth farther here than it would.",
  "TR 32.15 (interrupting a trial, laying the implement down): not modelled; the first release is the throw, and the javelin may not be gripped again.",
  "TR 32.14 names no fall: here a fall ends the trial as a failure, as in every task of this benchmark; the trial ends when the robot has left the runway correctly and stands.",
  "TR 25.17 (one minute for a trial): the whole trial, leaving the runway included, has 60 s.",
  "The qualifying mark of 20.0 m is this benchmark's (a boys' school standard for the 800 g javelin); the rule book only measures."
 ],
 "texts": {
  "sentence": "Throw the javelin held by its cord in your right gripper over the shoulder from behind the arc so that its head strikes the ground first, inside the sector, at least 20.0 m from the arc, then leave the runway behind the arc and stand.",
  "rules": "The men's javelin (0.8 kg, 2.65 m long, its centre of gravity 0.98 m from the tip, a metal head of 0.29 m; its cord grip, 0.16 m long and 0.029 m across, covers the centre of gravity) starts held at its cord between the two jaws of your right hand, a two-jaw gripper that holds only by friction: the javelin can slip along or turn in the jaws, and drop. It is held while both jaws touch the cord (breaks of at most 0.02 s are ignored); the release is the end of the hold: the last moment both jaws touched the cord, however it ends (opened, slipped or dropped). Until the release the javelin may not touch your left arm or hand (the javelin is held with one hand). The runway is 4.0 m wide between the inner edges of its side lines and 30.0 m long; it ends at the arc, a white strip whose inner edge is a circle of 8.0 m radius round a point on the runway's centre line; short white lines run outwards from the arc's two ends at right angles to the side lines. The sector is bounded by the two lines from the arc's centre through the arc's ends (28.96 degrees between them). The throw: at the release the javelin's centre of gravity must be at least 0.0 m higher than your right shoulder joint. There is one release: after it the javelin may not be held again (both jaws on the cord once more) and no part of you may touch it, except that your right arm and its gripper may still touch it during the first 0.2 s (the shaft sliding out through the jaws). The throw is valid only if the head touches the ground before any other part of the javelin, at a point inside the sector lines (on a line is out). It is measured from that point to the inner edge of the arc along the line through the arc's centre. You succeed when a valid throw measures at least 20.0 m and you have then left the runway correctly and stood for 1.0 s. Leaving correctly: after the javelin has landed, your first touch of a side line or of the ground outside the runway is behind the arc's end lines, or you touch the ground 4.0 m or more behind the arc's ends. Standing: only your feet on the ground, the torso within about 20 degrees of upright, the pelvis slower than 0.35 m/s. You fail if, at any time before that, any part of you touches the arc, its end lines or the ground beyond them; if you touch a side line or the ground outside the runway (also behind its rear end) before the javelin has landed; if your chest is turned more than 135.0 degrees away from the throwing direction before the release (your back to the arc); if the javelin touches your left arm or hand before the release; if it is released lower than the shoulder; if it is held again, or touched, after the release (but for the grace above); if it does not land head first, lands outside the sector or short of 20.0 m; if you fall (your pelvis lower than 0.5 m above the ground, or any part of you other than your feet on the ground); or after 60 s. Where you touch the ground is judged on the points of contact.",
  "venue": "You stand on the runway's centre line facing the sector (+x), your pelvis 2.0 m behind the middle of the arc; the arc's centre is 6.0 m behind you, its ends are 0.25 m behind its middle and 2.0 m to either side, and the runway reaches 28 m behind you. Your right arm hangs down, its gripper's fingers pointing down; the javelin lies level across the gripper's closed jaws, its cord between them, pointing forwards (the tip 0.98 m ahead of the jaws). Still air. The jaws open when your right grip command goes to 0 (at most 375 mm/s).",
  "difficulty": "20.0 m asks for a release at about 14 m/s near 35 to 40 degrees with the javelin pointing along its flight, or it will not come down head first; the H1-2 has no running policy and its arm motors give 40 N m at the shoulder. Nothing holds the 2.65 m javelin but two jaws 34 mm wide on its cord: a whip of the arm turns it in them, and the jaws must let go of it at the right moment, above the shoulder, on a body that has to stay on its feet behind the arc."
 },
